WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.534 --> 00:00:05.000
-Our first guest tonight
is a Grammy Award winning actor

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:07.601
currently starring
in "Gutenberg! The Musical!,"

00:00:07.601 --> 00:00:10.267
which is on Broadway now
at the James Earl Jones Theater.

00:00:10.267 --> 00:00:12.434
Please welcome to the show
for the very first time,

00:00:12.434 --> 00:00:13.934
Josh Gad, everybody.

00:00:13.934 --> 00:00:16.100
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:23.567
♪♪

00:00:23.567 --> 00:00:31.033
♪♪

00:00:31.033 --> 00:00:33.234
Welcome to the show.

00:00:33.234 --> 00:00:37.133
-Fred, I so want to go
to a 1000s party now.

00:00:37.133 --> 00:00:40.567
I really I have to go
to an airport this week and go.

00:00:40.567 --> 00:00:41.667
-I'm so happy you're here.

00:00:41.667 --> 00:00:42.934
And I can't believe

00:00:42.934 --> 00:00:43.968
you've never been here.
-I'm so happy that I --

00:00:43.968 --> 00:00:46.434
No, but that's your fault.
-Yeah.

00:00:46.434 --> 00:00:48.100
I can't invite myself
on your show.

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:49.467
I've tried.
-Yeah.

00:00:49.467 --> 00:00:52.167
-But it doesn't work that way,
from what I understand.

00:00:52.167 --> 00:00:54.334
-Well, I'm very glad we've
rectified the situation.

00:00:54.334 --> 00:00:57.200
And I hope this is a situation
where you come back and back.

00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:58.667
-I will be here.

00:00:58.667 --> 00:01:01.734
-By the way, I'm so grateful
for the fact that you were here

00:01:01.734 --> 00:01:03.634
on a night where
you're doing a show on Broadway.

00:01:03.634 --> 00:01:04.901
-I'm going to -- I'm going
to leave here

00:01:04.901 --> 00:01:06.267
and I'm going to dance
for another audience.

00:01:06.267 --> 00:01:07.267
-Yeah.

00:01:07.267 --> 00:01:09.400
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:12.767
But for now,
I speak to you.

00:01:12.767 --> 00:01:14.634
-Does this serve in any way,

00:01:14.634 --> 00:01:16.234
shape or form
as a warmup for you?

00:01:16.234 --> 00:01:17.667
-This is my warmup.
-Okay.

00:01:17.667 --> 00:01:19.534
-Do you guys want to see
what I do for a warmup?

00:01:19.534 --> 00:01:20.534
-Yes.

00:01:20.534 --> 00:01:21.701
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:21.701 --> 00:01:22.901
-It's very --

00:01:22.901 --> 00:01:26.133
So I start off
by doing stretches like this.

00:01:26.133 --> 00:01:27.167
-Okay.
Okay. Gotcha.

00:01:27.167 --> 00:01:28.701
Which, by the way,
I'm pretty limber

00:01:28.701 --> 00:01:30.667
for a guy my size.

00:01:30.667 --> 00:01:31.667
[ Laughter ]

00:01:31.667 --> 00:01:32.801
So I do this.
-Okay.

00:01:32.801 --> 00:01:33.901
-And then I do lip trills.

00:01:33.901 --> 00:01:34.968
You guys know
what lip trills are?

00:01:34.968 --> 00:01:36.901
Brrr.
-Okay.

00:01:36.901 --> 00:01:39.367
-Lip trills. None of you
should do that.

00:01:39.367 --> 00:01:41.834
And then -- And then
I sing '80s pop ballads.

00:01:41.834 --> 00:01:43.167
-You do?
-I do.

00:01:43.167 --> 00:01:44.634
-And have you found
historically,

00:01:44.634 --> 00:01:47.200
through a life on Broadway,
that that's helpful for you?

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:50.501
-There's so much falsetto
in '80s songs for some reason.

00:01:50.501 --> 00:01:51.801
So go high.

00:01:51.801 --> 00:01:54.667
I do "Take on Me" by A-ha,
I do some others.

00:01:54.667 --> 00:01:58.133
And Andrew Rannells, my costar,
brilliant costar, by the way.

00:01:58.133 --> 00:01:59.133
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:59.133 --> 00:02:01.567
Thank you.
He's -- He's not here.

00:02:01.567 --> 00:02:03.634
-He's not here. Let's not.
-You don't need a cheer for him.

00:02:03.634 --> 00:02:06.367
He -- But -- But he does
Sondheim songs.

00:02:06.367 --> 00:02:09.501
So this is very complicated.
-[ Laughs ]

00:02:09.501 --> 00:02:12.267
-Just infusion of different
sounds backstage

00:02:12.267 --> 00:02:13.534
when you come visit
our dressing room.

00:02:13.534 --> 00:02:16.167
-And you have
adjacent dressing rooms.

00:02:16.167 --> 00:02:18.100
-Yeah. Unfortunately
for Andrew, that is accurate.

00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:21.167
We have --
We're separated by a bathroom.

00:02:21.167 --> 00:02:22.701
A lot of bad things
happen in that bathroom

00:02:22.701 --> 00:02:25.000
before I go on stage,
but we have a bathroom,

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:26.667
and then our two
dressing rooms.

00:02:26.667 --> 00:02:28.234
-Are the walls thin enough

00:02:28.234 --> 00:02:29.968
that you don't hear
each other or you do?

00:02:29.968 --> 00:02:32.133
-No, they're frighteningly thin?
-Yeah.

00:02:32.133 --> 00:02:34.300
-They're frighteningly thin,
uncomfortably thin walls.

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:37.434
-Everything about Broadway
dressing rooms is frightening

00:02:37.434 --> 00:02:39.033
compared to what
your expectations are.

00:02:39.033 --> 00:02:42.167
They are.
-If you love --

00:02:42.167 --> 00:02:44.934
If you love,
like, getting sinus infections,

00:02:44.934 --> 00:02:46.100
it's a great place
to hang out.

00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:47.701
-Yeah.
-There's a lot --

00:02:47.701 --> 00:02:50.133
There's a lot of dust
mites in there, which is great.

00:02:50.133 --> 00:02:51.467
-Yeah. You know,
when I've been lucky enough

00:02:51.467 --> 00:02:53.934
to go backstage to say hi
to friends and shows, like,

00:02:53.934 --> 00:02:55.501
it's so cramped.
-It's very cramped.

00:02:55.501 --> 00:02:57.834
You feel bad visiting.
It's like, if --

00:02:57.834 --> 00:03:00.501
-You're making me feel bad for
having to go there after this,

00:03:00.501 --> 00:03:01.701
actually, like,
now I'm starting...

00:03:01.701 --> 00:03:03.901
-It's like going to visit
a friend in an elevator.

00:03:03.901 --> 00:03:05.434
-Yeah, yeah.
-It's before they go up.

00:03:05.434 --> 00:03:07.267
-That is 100 --
Except you go nowhere.

00:03:07.267 --> 00:03:09.000
It doesn't even go up and down.
-Yeah.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:12.033
-You just sit and have to listen
to people going to the bathroom.

00:03:12.033 --> 00:03:14.000
-Now, it is crazy.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:17.100
I mean, you and Andrew
are very young men before you --

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:18.467
When you met...
-Before.

00:03:18.467 --> 00:03:19.868
-Before...
[ Laughter ]

00:03:19.868 --> 00:03:20.934
You know how time works,
right?

00:03:20.934 --> 00:03:22.734
I'm not breaking
any news here, but...

00:03:22.734 --> 00:03:23.968
-I was going to say,
"That's so sweet."

00:03:23.968 --> 00:03:26.667
Oh, he means 10 years ago.
Got it. Yeah.

00:03:26.667 --> 00:03:28.067
-You met
when you were very young.

00:03:28.067 --> 00:03:30.100
And now, obviously, you have
these very accomplished careers,

00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:33.300
and it does seem
certainly from the outside

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:35.234
that you genuinely
like one another.

00:03:35.234 --> 00:03:36.601
-We love each other.

00:03:36.601 --> 00:03:39.067
It took me a decade
to get back to Broadway.

00:03:39.067 --> 00:03:40.868
I, since "Book of Mormon,"

00:03:40.868 --> 00:03:43.601
I've been looking for something
to get back with,

00:03:43.601 --> 00:03:45.767
and I got sent this script
by Alex Timbers,

00:03:45.767 --> 00:03:47.200
a brilliant director.
-Yes.

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:48.467
And who also did
"Here Lies Love."

00:03:48.467 --> 00:03:49.801
-Yeah.
-Which is phenomenal.

00:03:49.801 --> 00:03:50.801
Everybody should go see
that show.

00:03:50.801 --> 00:03:52.767
It's phenomenal.

00:03:52.767 --> 00:03:56.400
And I love that they applauded
more for Andrew,

00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:57.667
who's not here than you,

00:03:57.667 --> 00:03:59.334
who is here and giving them
entertainment right now.

00:03:59.334 --> 00:04:01.234
-Andrew is such a...
-Shame on all of you.

00:04:01.234 --> 00:04:02.400
[ Laughter ]

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:04.367
But there -- there's
there's just something

00:04:04.367 --> 00:04:06.167
about finding the right thing.

00:04:06.167 --> 00:04:07.901
And Timbers called us up
and he said,

00:04:07.901 --> 00:04:09.434
"I think you guys
should read this.

00:04:09.434 --> 00:04:11.534
I directed it
Off-Broadway years ago.

00:04:11.534 --> 00:04:13.100
It's perfect
for the two of you."

00:04:13.100 --> 00:04:14.467
We read it and we fell
in love with it.

00:04:14.467 --> 00:04:16.634
It was exactly comedically
what I was looking for.

00:04:16.634 --> 00:04:19.267
-It's so great.
And now you -- your kids,

00:04:19.267 --> 00:04:22.267
I'm curious about your kids,
9 and 12, two girls.

00:04:22.267 --> 00:04:25.334
I assume they've been to many
Broadway shows at this point.

00:04:25.334 --> 00:04:26.934
-That assumption is incorrect.
-Wow.

00:04:26.934 --> 00:04:28.734
-They've been they've been
to many shows.

00:04:28.734 --> 00:04:30.667
Not -- not -- Well, I guess
on tour they've been.

00:04:30.667 --> 00:04:31.834
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:04:31.834 --> 00:04:32.868
Their favorite show
is "Matilda the Musical."

00:04:32.868 --> 00:04:34.067
-A great show.

00:04:34.067 --> 00:04:35.734
-They've seen that like
six times in London.

00:04:35.734 --> 00:04:39.267
And I took them
to a bunch of theater,

00:04:39.267 --> 00:04:42.133
but they came to see me...
-In this?

00:04:42.133 --> 00:04:44.834
-...in this, which was a very
unique experience.

00:04:44.834 --> 00:04:47.033
They've seen me in things like
"Frozen" and things like that.

00:04:47.033 --> 00:04:50.033
They've never seen me
on stage before.

00:04:50.033 --> 00:04:54.901
And so the girls came, and they
came to my opening and my 9,

00:04:54.901 --> 00:04:58.501
my 9-year-old kept waving
to me the entire show,

00:04:58.501 --> 00:05:00.300
and I was like, "Stop it,
you're" --

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:01.467
[ Laughter ]

00:05:01.467 --> 00:05:03.968
"You're embarrassing daddy."

00:05:03.968 --> 00:05:07.534
But my my older one, Ava,
was concerned

00:05:07.534 --> 00:05:11.834
because I got one of these
infamous sinus infections

00:05:11.834 --> 00:05:13.234
last week on opening night.

00:05:13.234 --> 00:05:15.934
So my voice started to go in

00:05:15.934 --> 00:05:17.567
in the at the beginning
of the show,

00:05:17.567 --> 00:05:19.567
and Ava started crying
because she could tell

00:05:19.567 --> 00:05:22.434
that my voice started going
and she looked at her mom

00:05:22.434 --> 00:05:24.200
and she's like, "Is,
"Dada going to be alright?

00:05:24.200 --> 00:05:26.968
Is Dada going to be?"
And backstage that night,

00:05:26.968 --> 00:05:29.667
my singing teacher,
Liz Caplan, was in the audience.

00:05:29.667 --> 00:05:31.667
So she comes backstage

00:05:31.667 --> 00:05:35.033
and she literally does like
a Mr. Miyagi from "Karate Kid"

00:05:35.033 --> 00:05:37.801
and starts getting in there,
and I could suddenly sing again.

00:05:37.801 --> 00:05:41.734
And my daughter looked like
Daniel Larusso's girlfriend

00:05:41.734 --> 00:05:43.834
at the end of "Karate Kid."
She's like, "Yeah!"

00:05:43.834 --> 00:05:45.234
[ Laughter ]

00:05:45.234 --> 00:05:48.033
She was so proud,
so happy.

00:05:48.033 --> 00:05:51.334
-I will say, when --
when, you know,

00:05:51.334 --> 00:05:53.334
I've had family
come to shows before,

00:05:53.334 --> 00:05:56.067
and my absolute nightmare
would be

00:05:56.067 --> 00:05:57.467
if I could see two of them,

00:05:57.467 --> 00:05:59.767
and one of them was waving
and the other one was crying.

00:05:59.767 --> 00:06:01.868
-Yes.
-I mean, that is like...

00:06:01.868 --> 00:06:03.767
When people like my wife
is always like, "Don't over --

00:06:03.767 --> 00:06:05.834
It's fine. A lot of friends
are going to come."

00:06:05.834 --> 00:06:07.267
I'm like, "But what
if I make eye contact?"

00:06:07.267 --> 00:06:09.801
That would just be like one --
Like, if my mom was waving.

00:06:09.801 --> 00:06:13.734
-It's too much pressure
to have both waving and crying

00:06:13.734 --> 00:06:15.701
and an audience
just wanting to laugh.

00:06:15.701 --> 00:06:17.667
-You play two people
with the dream

00:06:17.667 --> 00:06:18.934
of getting a Broadway show.

00:06:18.934 --> 00:06:21.734
So you're basically looking
for a producer that's --

00:06:21.734 --> 00:06:23.200
And you're sort
of pitching this idea.

00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:24.701
-Yeah.
So the idea of the show

00:06:24.701 --> 00:06:27.367
is that these guys have written
a show about Johann Gutenberg,

00:06:27.367 --> 00:06:28.868
the inventor
of the printing press.

00:06:28.868 --> 00:06:32.133
I like to call this show the
"Hamilton" of our generation.

00:06:32.133 --> 00:06:35.334
And they -- Even though,
technically, "Hamilton"

00:06:35.334 --> 00:06:37.634
is the "Hamilton"
of its own generation.

00:06:37.634 --> 00:06:40.501
So we, you know,
we play 20 characters each,

00:06:40.501 --> 00:06:44.567
Andrew and I,
we wear 150 hats to distinguish

00:06:44.567 --> 00:06:45.934
between each of these
characters,

00:06:45.934 --> 00:06:48.634
which is a nightmare for someone
as uncoordinated as I am.

00:06:48.634 --> 00:06:50.534
And -- And the two of us
are on stage

00:06:50.534 --> 00:06:52.501
throughout the entire thing.
-Singing, dancing.

00:06:52.501 --> 00:06:53.801
-Singing, dancing.
-Do you collapse when it's over?

00:06:53.801 --> 00:06:58.267
-Oh, my God,
I literally on two show days

00:06:58.267 --> 00:07:00.100
I will go backstage and sleep

00:07:00.100 --> 00:07:02.567
on what Andrew calls
my FEMA cot.

00:07:02.567 --> 00:07:06.601
I have this little cot
that looks really upsetting

00:07:06.601 --> 00:07:09.400
and -- and it doesn't
quite fit my body on it,

00:07:09.400 --> 00:07:12.334
so I just sort of like
hang over the edge of it.

00:07:12.334 --> 00:07:15.234
But I sleep on my FEMA cot,
I get a little nap in,

00:07:15.234 --> 00:07:17.901
I have a nice little dinner,
and then I get back to it.

00:07:17.901 --> 00:07:21.000
But, you know, those two show
days, they're, it's a lot.

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:22.167
-You you mentioned "Frozen."

00:07:22.167 --> 00:07:23.567
I told my kids
you were coming on tonight.

00:07:23.567 --> 00:07:24.868
-Aww.
-They were very excited.

00:07:24.868 --> 00:07:27.667
But then it always --
This moment of sadness hits

00:07:27.667 --> 00:07:30.033
when they realize it's not
the actual animated character.

00:07:30.033 --> 00:07:31.400
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:07:31.400 --> 00:07:33.300
-And at that moment
of sadness hits my daughters

00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:34.601
every day when I come home.

00:07:34.601 --> 00:07:35.601
[ Laughter ]

00:07:35.601 --> 00:07:36.734
Where they go,

00:07:36.734 --> 00:07:38.033
this isn't what we
signed up for.

00:07:38.033 --> 00:07:39.734
-But at least on sunny days,
they don't have to worry.

00:07:39.734 --> 00:07:41.000
-[ Laughs ]
-That's the good news.

00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:42.767
Hey, what a delight.
We got to do it again soon.

00:07:42.767 --> 00:07:43.767
-I love you asked.
-It was so great to have you

00:07:43.767 --> 00:07:44.868
back in New York City?

00:07:44.868 --> 00:07:46.868
-I love you guys.
Josh Gad.

00:07:46.868 --> 00:07:48.200
"Gutenberg! The Musical!,"

00:07:48.200 --> 00:07:49.934
now playing
at the James Earl Jones Theatre.

00:07:49.934 --> 00:07:51.000
Go see it and stick around.

00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:52.300
We'll be right back
with Joe Paris.

